@startuml proto-sr04-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-sr04-frame.puml - (b) the HC-SR04 exchange, and the one number that is
' the answer.
'
' HC-SR04 is the only act in this family whose OUTPUT IS A QUANTITY rather than a
' waveform. So the "frame" is a measurement envelope - trigger, recovery, echo
' pulse - and the payload is a pulse whose WIDTH is the distance.
' ===========================================================================

title HC-SR04 - the exchange, and the conversion that is the act\n<font size=12>STATUS: RED; the conversion is EXACT on the one measurement that ran (1160 us -> 199 mm)</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<host>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<dev>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<red>> {
  BackgroundColor #FADADA
  BorderColor #AA3030
}
skinparam rectangle<<open>> {
  BackgroundColor #EFEFEF
  BorderColor #999999
}

rectangle "TRIGGER\n<b>host</b>, pin 6\n\n<b>10 us</b> minimum\n\n<b>MEASURED 601 clocks\n= 10.0167 us</b>\nthe act derives\n<b>4 * 149 + 4 = 600</b>" as T <<red>>
rectangle "RECOVERY\n<b>the sensor settles</b>\n\nSR_RECOV_LEN = 117\nthe stated 2 010 us\n\n<b>its absence was a real\ndefect; its presence did\nnot make measurement 1\nhappen</b>" as R <<dev>>
rectangle "8 x 40 kHz BURSTS\n<b>the sensor</b>\n\nthe device emits a\nchirp and then holds ECHO\nhigh for the round trip\n\n<b>the firmware never\nlooks at these</b> - it\ncannot resynchronise to\na burst it did not send" as B <<dev>>
rectangle "ECHO\n<b>sensor</b>, pin 5, RELEASED\nand READ BACK\n\n<b>HIGH FOR THE ROUND TRIP</b>\n\n<b>THE ANSWER IS THE WIDTH,\brather than a level.</b>\n\nwidth = 2 * distance / 0.343" as E <<dev>>

rectangle "MEASURE\nt in us\n\n<b>the 1 us counter as a\nDIFFERENCE, not the flag</b>" as M <<host>>
rectangle "CONVERT\n<b>mm = us * 11/64</b>\n\n<b>EXACT</b>\n\n= 11q + floor(11r/64)\nwith us = 64q + r\n\nno multiply\nno divide\nno approximation" as C <<host>>
rectangle "BANK\n\n<b>dmem[10] = 01</b>\n\n<b>expected 02</b>\n\nmeasurement 0 banked\nmeasurement 1 never ran" as K <<red>>

T -[#3A7]-> R
R -[#3A7]-> B
B -[#3A7]-> E
E -[#3A7]-> M
M -[#3A7]-> C
C -[#3A7]-> K

note top of T
  <b>WHY THE TRIGGER IS 601 CLOCKS AND NOT 600 IS WORTH
  NAMING EVEN THOUGH IT IS NOT THE FAULT.</b>
  ...
  The device asks for 10 us MINIMUM, so the extra clock is harmless to a real
  sensor. The check is an EQUALITY anyway, because the act claims the COUNT and
  not a datasheet floor - and that is the same discipline the WS2812 act uses when
  a 74-clock cell clears every window in print.
  ...
  601 against 600 is a ONE-INSTRUCTION error, and a one-instruction error of
  exactly this shape is what this family has found six times. It is named here as
  the most likely place to look for the lost second measurement; it is NOT claimed
  to be the cause, because the five failing checks do not say so.
end note

note right of E
  <b>WHY THE ECHO IS CAPTURED AND NOT SAMPLED,</b> which is
  what makes this act safe at 4 m.

  A receiver that samples the level and decides "there is
  something there" has to poll, and a poll loop that is
  long relative to the distance reading adds its own
  uncertainty to every sample. A receiver that measures
  the WIDTH does not care when it looked: the two edges
  define the interval, and the interval is the answer.
  ...
  The same reason the counter is RESET at the rising edge
  and READ at the falling edge rather than subtracted:
  no 16-bit subtract, and this machine has no carry flag,
  so a subtract is a four-case analysis on bit 7 and a
  compare is the same analysis again. <b>One reset removes
  both.</b>
end note

note bottom of C
  <b>THE IDENTITY, and why it removes the multiply the plan
  budgeted for.</b>
  ...
    us = 64q + r
    us * 11 / 64  =  11q + 11r/64  =  11q + floor(11r/64)
  ...
  <b>EXACTLY, because 11q is an integer.</b> So the conversion is a multiply by
  eleven of a ten-bit number, a multiply by eleven of a six-bit number, and one
  add. The plan for this act assumed a 16x16 multiply; this identity removes it,
  along with the division and the approximation the plan expected the division to
  cost.
  ...
  <b>A MULTIPLY BY ELEVEN IS FOUR DOUBLINGS AND TWO ADDS:</b>
  x -> 2x -> 4x -> 5x -> 10x -> 11x. No loop, no counter, no
  second temporary - the multiplicand stays in Q throughout.
  ...
  <b>No 16-bit overflow is possible anywhere:</b> the largest value involved is
  11 * (23000 >> 6) = 3949, the intermediates are 8q = 2872 and 10q = 3590, and
  the second term is 11 * 63 = 693. Recorded rather than assumed, because an
  unnoticed overflow in the high byte is invisible from the outside - the answer
  would still be a plausible millimetre figure.
end note

note bottom of M
  <b>11/64 IS +0.22 % LONG AGAINST THE SPEED OF SOUND, AND THAT
  ERROR BELONGS TO THE CONSTANT.</b>
  ...
    t (us) = 2 * d (mm) / 0.343 mm/us   ->   d (mm) = t * 11/64
  ...
  The arithmetic below is EXACT for 11/64, which is a different and checkable
  claim from "the range is right to two parts in a thousand". Only the first can be
  asserted as an equality, and the testbench asserts the first: "us*11/64 is exact
  and this is an equality". A page that reported the accuracy of the ranging would
  be reporting a property of a constant that was chosen to divide, not of the
  firmware.
end note

legend bottom
  |= measurement |= echo | expected | firmware banked | result | status |=
  | 0 | <b>1 160 us</b> | 199 mm | 1 160 us | <b>199 mm</b> | <b>PASS - the conversion is exact</b> |
  | 1 | 5 816 us | 999 mm | 1 160 us | X mm | <b>FAIL - the second measurement never ran</b> |
  | trigger | - | 600 clocks | 601 clocks | - | <b>FAIL - one clock long</b> |
  | count | - | 2 | 1 | - | <b>FAIL - dmem[10] = 01</b> |

  <b>Why the two targets are 1 160 us and 5 816 us.</b> The small one fits in
  twelve bits and the large one does not, so the pair exercises the ten-bit path
  <b>and</b> the high byte. A single target at the near end would prove the
  conversion works and say nothing about the case that could overflow - and an
  overflow in the high byte is invisible from the outside, because the answer would
  still be a plausible millimetre figure.
end legend

@enduml
